SUMMARY:The lab of the future: How AI is changing the way we develop new materials
DESCRIPTION:Register now! \nWhat if AI could help uncover new materials that improve drug delivery\, support sustainable agriculture and address environmental challenges? \nBy combining artificial intelligence\, robotics and automated experimentation\, self-driving laboratories are transforming the way new materials and chemical products are discovered. Join Professor Frank Gu (ChemE) as he shares how AI-powered research is accelerating innovation in areas such as drug delivery\, agricultural technologies and water treatment\, enabling researchers to identify promising solutions faster and more efficiently than ever before. \nJoin us on December 2\, 2026\, at our monthly Skule™ Lunch & Learn. \n\nBy registering for the Skule™ Lunch & Learn event\, you could potentially earn Continuing Professional Development (CPD) credits. CPDs are essential for professional engineers and limited license holders to renew their licenses annually through the PEO PEAK Program. If you’re wondering whether this event offering qualifies you for CPD credits\, click here to learn more. \n\nAbout the speaker\nProfessor Frank Gu\nProfessor Frank Gu is the Director of the Institute for Water Innovation\, Co-Director\, Formulation Self-Driving Lab\, Acceleration Consortium and a professor in the Department of Chemical Engineering and Applied Chemistry\, where he holds an NSERC Senior Industrial Research Chair in Nanotechnology Engineering. A leading nanotechnology engineer\, Dr. Gu has advanced the development of functional nanomaterials with applications in healthcare and environmental sustainability. \nHis research spans nanomedicine\, drug delivery\, AI-powered materials discovery and water treatment. He has helped develop innovative nanomaterials for treating industrial wastewater and advancing drug delivery for eye diseases. In 2015\, he founded H2nanO Inc.\, a company that commercializes energy-efficient water treatment technologies for industrial applications. Throughout his career\, Dr. Gu has mentored more than 200 students and research associates\, published over 110 peer-reviewed papers\, and holds more than 60 issued or pending patents.
URL:https://www.engineering.utoronto.ca/event/the-lab-of-the-future-how-ai-is-changing-the-way-we-develop-new-materials/
LOCATION:Online
CATEGORIES:Lectures, seminars and workshops
END:VEVENT
